# {{.ProjectName}}

A new project generated with `nucleus new`. This is an empty skeleton: a
composition root (`main.go`), configuration (`nucleus.yml`), and a place for
SQL migrations (`migrations/`). It has no application features yet — you add
those by writing **modules**.

## Run

```
go run .
```

`nucleus new` already resolved the database driver ({{.DriverModule}}) and
wrote `go.sum`; if the project was scaffolded with `--offline`, run
`go get {{.DriverModule}} && go mod tidy` first. The app starts on the port
in `nucleus.yml` (default `8080`). With no modules mounted it exposes only
the framework's built-in endpoints (e.g. `/healthz`).

## Add your first feature

Features live in self-contained modules that you mount in `main.go`. The
CLI writes one and mounts it:

```
nucleus generate module notes --mount
go run .
```

That gives you `internal/notes/` — model and storage, a REST controller,
the module with its own policy rows, CSRF exemption and embedded
migrations (applied on start), a page template and a test — plus the
`Mount(notes.Module())` line in `main.go`:

```go
nucleus.New().
    FromConfigFile("nucleus.yml").
    Mount(notes.Module()).   // your module
    Start()
```

`go test ./...` runs the module's test against an in-process application.
For the same shape written by hand, see the **`examples/mvc_api`** reference
application in the Nucleus repository and the **Quickstart** in the
documentation.

### Admin UI (optional)

The framework core ships no admin panel. To add one, pull in the Orbit module
(`github.com/jcsvwinston/orbit`) and mount it like any other module:
`Mount(orbit.Module(orbit.Config{...}))`. Orbit manages its own login gate
and bootstrap credentials through that `orbit.Config` — see its README.

## Container image

`Dockerfile` and `.dockerignore` build this project into a small image: a
multi-stage build, a static binary (`CGO_ENABLED=0` — every Nucleus driver
is pure Go), a distroless runtime base pinned by digest, and a process that
runs as uid 65532 instead of root. `/data` is the one path that user can
write to; everything else in the image belongs to root.

```
docker build -t {{.ProjectName}} .
{{- if eq .Database "sqlite"}}
docker run --rm -p {{.Port}}:{{.Port}} \
    -v {{.ProjectName}}-data:/data \
    -e NUCLEUS_DATABASES__DEFAULT__URL=sqlite:///data/app.db \
    {{.ProjectName}}
{{- else}}
docker run --rm -p {{.Port}}:{{.Port}} {{.ProjectName}}
{{- end}}
```

`nucleus doctor --check image` re-reads that file after you edit it and
reports the settings that are easy to get wrong: a runtime stage back on
root, a base image on a mutable tag, cgo left on, credentials in `ENV`.

## Migrations

A generated module embeds its own migrations. For project-level SQL, put
migrations under `migrations/` (e.g. `000001_create_notes.up.sql` /
`.down.sql`) and apply them with the CLI:

```
nucleus migrate --config nucleus.yml --migrations migrations up
```
